  "background": "entertainer",
  "backstory": "Born beneath the billowing canvas of a traveling carnival, the man now known only as the Masque spent his youth perfecting the art of the blade not for war, but for wonder. As the star of the Silver-Tongued Troupe, he was a master of stage-combat and choreographed duels, his movements a fluid dance that left audiences breathless from the High Pass to the Southern Isles. He never spoke during his performances, letting the rhythmic whistle of his rapier and the acrobatic grace of his footwork tell stories of ancient heroes and legendary triumphs. To the world, he was a phantom of the footlights, a nameless figure whose identity was entirely subsumed by the colorful costumes and the roar of the crowd.\n\nThe transition from performer to protector was forged in fire and blood when a marauding warband descended upon the troupe’s camp under the cover of a moonless night. Amidst the screams of his kin and the scent of burning cedar, the dancer found that the intricate patterns of the stage were lethally effective when translated to the chaos of a real skirmish. With a discarded soldier’s longsword in hand, he defended the few survivors with an elegance that surpassed his training, realizing in the heat of the slaughter that the world did not need more plays—it needed a wall against the darkness. He discarded his sequins for scarred leather and cold steel, trading the hollow applause of the nobility for the grim, silent satisfaction of a blade well-aimed.\n\nNow, he wanders the fractured realms as a sellsword with no past and no face, his true name buried beneath the charred ruins of his former life. He is driven by a singular, burning purpose: to track down the shadowed patron who orchestrated the raid on his troupe, a figure he caught only a glimpse of through the smoke of the massacre. Every tavern he visits and every monster he slays is a step toward unmasking the villain behind his tragedy. He fights not for personal glory, but because he views the world as a grand stage where the villains have been allowed to write the script for too long, and he is determined to ensure the final act belongs to justice.",
